.TH INTEL\-SPEED\-SELECT 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
intel\-speed\-select \- enumerate and control Intel Speed Select Technology features
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B intel\-speed\-select
.RI [ options ]
.I feature command
.RI [ command-options ]
.br
.B intel\-speed\-select
.RI [ options ]
.I feature
.B \-\-help
.br
.B intel\-speed\-select
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-info
.br
.B intel\-speed\-select
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-oob
.br
.B intel\-speed\-select
.RB { \-\-help | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B intel\-speed\-select
enumerates and configures the Intel Speed Select Technology (Intel SST)
features of Intel server processors.
It works through the kernel's Intel Speed Select interface driver,
reached through
.IR \%/dev/\:isst_interface ,
and through the CPU hotplug, cpufreq and cgroup interfaces in sysfs.
.PP
The global
.I options
must come before
.IR feature :
option parsing stops at the first argument that is not an option.
Long global options may also be written with a single dash, and may be
abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix.
The
.I command-options
follow
.IR command .
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-cpu ,
a command is applied once to each power domain in the system (each
package, or each die of a package that has several dies, and on
platforms that have them, each power domain of a die), using the first
online CPU of the domain.
With
.BR \-\-cpu ,
the command is applied once for each listed CPU that is online; settings
that belong to a power domain then apply to the whole domain of that
CPU.
.PP
Results are printed as indented text, or as JSON with
.BR "\-\-format json" ,
grouped by package, die, power domain (where the platform has them) and
CPU.
They go to standard error unless
.B \-\-out
is given.
Before any result, the program prints a banner and the CPU model on
standard output.
.PP
.B intel\-speed\-select
has three levels of help:
.B \-\-help
alone lists the global options and features,
.I feature
.B \-\-help
lists the commands of a feature, and
.I feature command
.B \-\-help
describes a command and its
.IR command-options .
.SS Features and commands
.TP
.B perf\-profile
Intel SST Performance Profile (SST-PP): several performance levels, each
defining the set of CPUs that may be online and the base frequency those
CPUs can sustain.
Level 0 is the base level.
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B info
Print the properties of every performance level, or only of the level
given with
.BR \-l :
among others, the CPUs that may be online at that level, its base,
turbo and uncore frequencies, and whether base-freq and turbo-freq are
supported and enabled at that level.
.TP
.B get\-lock\-status
Print whether performance-level changes are locked.
.TP
.B get\-config\-levels
Print the highest performance level.
.TP
.B get\-config\-version
Print the performance profile (TDP) version.
.TP
.B get\-config\-enabled
Print whether performance profiles are enabled.
.TP
.B get\-config\-current\-level
Print the current performance level.
.TP
.B set\-config\-level
Switch to the level given with
.BR \-l ,
which is required.
With
.BR \-o ,
also bring online the CPUs of the new level and take the others
offline.
.RE
.TP
.B base\-freq
Intel SST Base Frequency (SST-BF): a higher base frequency on a fixed
set of high-priority cores in exchange for a lower base frequency on
the other cores.
Whether it is available depends on the current performance level.
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B info
Print the high-priority base frequency and CPUs, the low-priority base
frequency, the junction temperature limit and the thermal design power
of the level given with
.BR \-l ,
which is required.
.TP
.B enable
Enable SST-BF at the current performance level.
With
.BR \-a ,
first configure and enable core-power so that the high-priority CPUs
get priority (see
.BR \-\-auto ).
.TP
.B disable
Disable SST-BF.
With
.BR \-a ,
also disable core-power and restore the cpufreq minimum frequency.
.RE
.TP
.B turbo\-freq
Intel SST Turbo Frequency (SST-TF): higher turbo frequency limits for
high-priority cores at the cost of lower limits for low-priority cores.
Whether it is available depends on the current performance level.
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B info
Print, for the level given with
.BR \-l ,
which is required, the number of high-priority cores and their maximum
frequencies for each bucket, and the maximum frequencies to which
low-priority cores are clipped.
.B \-b
and
.B \-r
restrict the output to one bucket and to one TRL type.
.TP
.B enable
Enable SST-TF at the current performance level.
The command fails if turbo is disabled through
.IR \%/sys/\:devices/\:system/\:cpu/\:intel_pstate/\:no_turbo .
It enables core-power, enables SST-TF, and writes the turbo ratio
limits given with
.BR \-t ;
without
.BR \-t ,
every ratio limit is set to 0xff.
With
.BR \-a ,
it also makes the CPUs given with
.B \-\-cpu
high priority (see
.BR \-\-auto ).
.TP
.B disable
Disable SST-TF and restore the turbo ratio limits, to the value given
with
.B \-t
or else to those of the current performance level.
With
.BR \-a ,
also disable core-power.
.RE
.TP
.B core\-power
Intel SST Core Power (SST-CP): per-core priority through four classes of
service (CLOS 0 to 3), each with its own frequency limits and priority
parameters.
Priority is assigned per core, not per logical CPU.
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B info
Print whether core-power is supported and enabled, whether CLOS
prioritization is enabled, and the priority type.
.TP
.B enable
Enable CLOS-based prioritization, with the priority type given with
.BR \-p .
The program warns that cpufreq and core-power interfere with each other
if cpufreq is present.
.TP
.B disable
Disable CLOS-based prioritization.
On platforms that use the mailbox interface, this is refused while
turbo-freq is enabled.
.TP
.B config
Set the parameters of the CLOS given with
.BR \-c ,
using
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-w ,
.B \-n
and
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B get\-config
Print the parameters of the CLOS given with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B assoc
Associate the CPUs given with
.BR \-\-cpu ,
which is required, with the CLOS given with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B get\-assoc
Print the CLOS of each CPU given with
.BR \-\-cpu ,
which is required.
.RE
.TP
.B turbo\-mode
Turbo control through cpufreq, and access to the turbo ratio limits
(TRL) in MSR 0x1AD.
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B enable
Set the cpufreq
.I \%scaling_max_freq
of the CPUs to their
.IR \%cpuinfo_max_freq .
.TP
.B disable
Set the cpufreq
.I \%scaling_max_freq
of each online CPU to its
.IR \%base_frequency .
.TP
.B get\-trl
Print the turbo ratio limits.
.TP
.B set\-trl
Write the turbo ratio limits given with
.BR \-t ,
which is required.
.RE
.IP
.B enable
and
.B disable
act on all CPUs and ignore
.BR \-\-cpu ;
they do not change
.IR \%intel_pstate/\:no_turbo .
.SS Out-of-band mode
With
.BR \-\-oob ,
the program runs as a daemon that follows performance-level changes
made outside the operating system, for example by a management
controller, and brings CPUs online or offline to match.
.PP
Unless
.BR \-\-no\-daemon ,
.B \-\-debug
or
.B \-\-poll\-interval
is given, it detaches: it forks, starts a new session, closes all file
descriptors, changes to
.IR /tmp ,
and writes its process ID to
.IR \%/tmp/\:hfi\-events.pid ,
which it keeps locked; it exits if another process holds the lock.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-poll\-interval ,
it listens for CPU capability change events from the kernel's thermal
generic netlink family, and checks the power domain of the CPU named in
each event.
This requires a processor with Hardware Feedback Interface (HFI)
support; otherwise the daemon reports that the CPU does not support HFI
and exits.
With
.BR \-\-poll\-interval ,
it checks every power domain at that interval instead.
.PP
Whenever it finds a domain at a level other than the one it last
recorded for it (the first check of each domain counts as a change),
and the level is not locked, it brings the CPUs of the new level online
and takes the others offline, or isolates them with
.BR \-\-cgroupv2 .
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
stop the daemon.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global options
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all\-cpus\-online
Before running the command, bring every CPU online by writing 1 to its
.I online
file in sysfs, and delete the topology cache file.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cpu =\fIlist
Apply the command to the CPUs in
.I list
instead of to every power domain.
.I list
is a comma-separated list of CPU numbers and ranges; a range is written
.IB first \- last
or
.IB first .. last\fR,
as in
.BR 0,2,4\-7 .
At most 512 CPUs can be named.
CPUs that are not present are ignored, and offline CPUs are skipped.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Print debug messages on standard output.
With
.BR \-\-oob ,
also keep the daemon in the foreground.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format =\fIformat
Print results as JSON if
.I format
is
.BR json ,
and as text, the default, for any other value.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
Print platform information and exit: the interface API and driver
versions, whether mailbox and MMIO access are supported, which features
are supported, and whether performance-level changes are locked.
Options that follow
.B \-\-info
are not processed.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out =\fIfile
Write results to
.I file
instead of standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-pause= msec
Wait
.I msec
milliseconds before each mailbox command.
The default is 0.
A value that is 0 or not a number is ignored with a warning.
This option has no working short form: the usage summary lists
.BR \-p ,
but
.B \-p
is rejected as an ambiguous abbreviation of
.B \-\-pause
and
.BR \-\-poll\-interval .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-retry =\fIcount
Make up to
.I count
attempts at each mailbox command; the default is 3.
A value that is 0 or not a number is ignored with a warning.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the program version to the output file (standard error by
default) and exit.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-oob
Run in out-of-band mode, described above.
No
.I feature
or
.I command
is needed.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-daemon
With
.BR \-\-oob ,
stay in the foreground.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-poll\-interval =\fIseconds
With
.BR \-\-oob ,
check every power domain for level changes every
.I seconds
seconds instead of waiting for HFI events, and stay in the foreground.
A value that is 0 or not a number is an error.
The usage summary calls this option
.BR \-\-delay ,
but no such option exists.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-cgroupv2 =\fIarg
Where CPUs would be taken offline for a performance level
.RB ( "perf\-profile set\-config\-level \-o"
and
.BR \-\-oob ),
isolate them with a cgroup v2 cpuset partition instead.
The program enables the cpuset controller in
.IR \%/sys/\:fs/\:cgroup/\:cgroup.subtree_control ,
writes the CPUs that are not part of the level to
.I cpuset.cpus
of the cgroup
.IR \%/sys/\:fs/\:cgroup/ pkg \- die \- domain,
and sets its
.I cpuset.cpus.partition
to
.B isolated
(or
.B root
if
.B isolated
is refused).
Switching to level 0 sets the partition back to
.BR member .
If the cpuset controller cannot be enabled, CPUs are taken offline as
usual.
.B \-g
takes no argument; the long form requires one, which is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-cpu0\-workaround =\fIarg
Never take CPU 0 offline; isolate it with cgroup v2, as for
.BR \-\-cgroupv2 ,
instead, and remove that isolation when CPU 0 would be brought online.
The program does this even without
.B \-u
when CPU 0 has no
.I online
file.
.B \-u
takes no argument; the long form requires one, which is ignored.
.PP
.B \-\-pause
and
.B \-\-retry
apply only to platforms that use the mailbox interface (interface API
version 1).
.SS Command options
Several command options share their letter with a global option
.RB ( \-a ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-o ,
.B \-r
and
.BR \-w );
an option means the global one before
.I feature
and the command one after
.IR command .
Numeric values must be written in decimal, except for
.BR \-\-trl .
A malformed value makes the program print a message and exit.
Unknown options and surplus arguments are reported and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-level =\fIlevel
Performance level.
Required by
.BR "perf\-profile set\-config\-level" ,
.B base\-freq info
and
.BR "turbo\-freq info" ;
optional for
.BR "perf\-profile info" ,
which prints every level without it.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-online =\fIarg
For
.BR "perf\-profile set\-config\-level" :
after switching level, bring online each CPU in the new level's CPU list,
setting its cpufreq
.I \%scaling_max_freq
to its base frequency if it is lower or if turbo is disabled, and take
every other CPU of the power domain offline, or isolate it with
.BR \-\-cgroupv2 .
It also sets the uncore frequency limits in
.I \%/sys/\:devices/\:system/\:cpu/\:intel_uncore_frequency/
to those of the new level, except on processors whose CPUID extended
family is 4.
.B \-o
takes no argument; the long form requires one, which is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-auto
For
.B base\-freq enable
and
.BR "turbo\-freq enable" ,
also set up core-power priorities.
For base-freq, the high-priority CPUs reported by
.B base\-freq info
are associated with CLOS 0 and all other CPUs with CLOS 3; the minimum
frequency of CLOS 0 is set to the high-priority base frequency and that
of CLOS 1 to 3 to the low-priority base frequency; core-power is enabled
with ordered priority; and the cpufreq scaling limits of the online CPUs
are adjusted.
For turbo-freq, the CPUs given with
.B \-\-cpu
and their thread siblings are associated with CLOS 0 and every other
online CPU with CLOS 3.
For
.B base\-freq disable
and
.BR "turbo\-freq disable" ,
also disable core-power; base-freq also sets each online CPU's cpufreq
.I \%scaling_min_freq
back to its
.IR \%cpuinfo_min_freq .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket =\fIbucket
For
.BR "turbo\-freq info" :
print only this bucket.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-trl\-type =\fItype
For
.BR "turbo\-freq info" :
print only the high-priority frequencies of this TRL type:
.B sse
(TRL level 0),
.B avx2
(level 1) or
.B avx512
(level 2).
Any other value is an error.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trl =\fIvalue
Turbo ratio limits, as a hexadecimal number with a
.B 0x
prefix, written to MSR 0x1AD; each byte is one ratio limit.
Used by
.BR "turbo\-freq enable" ,
.B turbo\-freq disable
and
.BR "turbo\-mode set\-trl" .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clos =\fIclos
CLOS number, 0 to 3, for
.BR "core\-power config" ,
.B get\-config
and
.BR assoc .
Required by these commands.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-epp =\fIepp
For
.BR "core\-power config" :
energy performance preference of the CLOS, 0 to 15.
If it is missing or out of range, 0 is used.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-weight =\fIweight
For
.BR "core\-power config" :
proportional priority weight of the CLOS, 0 to 15.
If it is missing or out of range, 0 is used.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-min =\fImhz
For
.BR "core\-power config" :
minimum frequency of the CLOS in MHz.
The default is 0.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max =\fImhz
For
.BR "core\-power config" :
maximum frequency of the CLOS in MHz.
The default is ratio 0xff, which the program reports as the maximum
frequency.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-desired =\fImhz
Accepted, but
.B core\-power config
reports that a desired frequency is not supported and uses 0.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-priority =\fItype
For
.BR "core\-power enable" :
priority type,
.B 0
for proportional (the default) or
.B 1
for ordered.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Describe the command and its options, and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/dev/\:isst_interface
Intel Speed Select interface device.
.TP
.I \%/var/\:run/\:isst_cpu_topology.dat
Cache of the package, die and core IDs of every CPU, created when it
does not exist and used for CPUs whose topology is not in sysfs, such as
offline CPUs.
Deleted by
.BR \-\-all\-cpus\-online .
.TP
.IR \%/sys/\:devices/\:system/\:cpu/\:cpu N/
The CPU's
.I online
file, its
.I topology
directory, and in its
.I cpufreq
directory
.IR \%base_frequency ,
.IR \%scaling_min_freq ,
.IR \%scaling_max_freq ,
.I \%cpuinfo_min_freq
and
.IR \%cpuinfo_max_freq .
.TP
.I \%/sys/\:devices/\:system/\:cpu/\:intel_pstate/\:no_turbo
Read to check whether turbo is disabled.
.TP
.I \%/sys/\:devices/\:system/\:cpu/\:intel_uncore_frequency/
Uncore frequency limits, set by
.BR "perf\-profile set\-config\-level \-o" .
.TP
.I \%/sys/\:fs/\:cgroup/
cgroup v2 hierarchy used by
.B \-\-cgroupv2
and
.BR \-\-cpu0\-workaround .
.TP
.I /proc/cpuinfo
Read to identify Cascade Lake-N processors.
.TP
.I \%/tmp/\:hfi\-events.pid
Process ID and lock file of the out-of-band daemon.
.SH NOTES
If the effective user ID is not 0, the program tries to open
.I \%/dev/\:isst_interface
for reading and writing.
If it cannot, it prints
.RB \(dq "Must run as root" \(dq
and exits.
If it can, it runs in read-only mode, in which the commands that change
settings
.RB ( set\-config\-level ,
.BR enable ,
.BR disable ,
.BR config ,
.B assoc
and
.BR set\-trl )
fail with
.RB \(dq "Insufficient privileges" \(dq.
.PP
The program needs
.IR \%/dev/\:isst_interface ,
which the kernel's Intel Speed Select interface drivers provide
.RB \%( CONFIG_INTEL_SPEED_SELECT_INTERFACE );
if it cannot open the device, it reports that the drivers are not
loaded and exits.
This check, the privilege check and the check of the processor model
happen before the options are processed, so even
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
need a supported system.
.PP
Cascade Lake processors (CPU model 0x55, stepping 6 or 7) are supported
only in the Cascade Lake-N models Xeon 6252N, 6230N and 5218N, which are
identified from
.IR /proc/cpuinfo ;
on other processors of that model and stepping, the program exits with
the message
.RB \(dq "Invalid CPU model" \(dq.
On the supported models the Intel SST features are configured by the
BIOS, and only
.B perf\-profile info
(level 0 only),
.BR "base\-freq info" ,
.B base\-freq enable
and
.B base\-freq disable
are available;
.B \-\-info
is not.
There,
.B base\-freq enable
sets the cpufreq
.I \%scaling_min_freq
and
.I \%scaling_max_freq
of each CPU to its high- or low-priority base frequency, and
.B base\-freq disable
sets them back to
.I \%cpuinfo_min_freq
and
.IR \%cpuinfo_max_freq ;
neither changes the hardware configuration.
.PP
On Skylake-based platforms (CPU model 0x55), core-power always uses
ordered priority: the command options
.BR \-e ,
.B \-w
and
.B \-p 0
are rejected there.
.PP
The exit status does not reliably indicate success: many errors,
including an unknown feature or command, end with status 0, while
.B \-\-help
and a missing feature or command end with status 1.
.PP
The program's own version is v1.25.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print platform information:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# intel\-speed\-select \-\-info
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the properties of performance level 0, then switch to level 4,
taking offline the CPUs that level 4 does not include:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# intel\-speed\-select perf\-profile info \-l 0
# intel\-speed\-select perf\-profile set\-config\-level \-l 4 \-o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Associate CPU 10 with CLOS 3:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# intel\-speed\-select \-c 10 core\-power assoc \-c 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Enable turbo-freq with CPUs 12 and 13 as high priority, writing the
result as JSON to a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# intel\-speed\-select \-f json \-o tf.json \-c 12,13 turbo\-freq enable \-a
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR turbostat (8),
.BR \%x86_energy_perf_policy (8)
.PP
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:pm/\:intel\-speed\-select.rst
in the Linux source.
